WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.609
It's been just under three weeks since I

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:07.470
uploaded my 10,000 subscriber's thank you

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.120
video. Now here we are at the momentous

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.700
100,000 milestone. A lot of people have

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.200
asked for a behind-the-scenes look at how

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.910
I make my videos. Before we get started,

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.500
let me just take a moment to thank all

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.090
of you amazing people for sticking with

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.290
Second Thought and supporting the

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.329
channel. I'm also going to use this video

00:00:21.329 --> 00:00:23.130
as a Q&amp;A, so if you have a question that

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.239
I haven't answered by the end of the

00:00:24.239 --> 00:00:25.950
video, leave me a comment below and i'll

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.630
respond to you as quickly as I can.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.180
Now, on to the main event. Every Second

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.649
Thought video starts as a simple idea;

00:00:31.649 --> 00:00:33.840
When something interests me, I write it

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.579
down on my ever-expanding list of video

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:37.920
topics. At any given time, I have about

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.449
two or three months worth of videos to

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:40.170
choose from.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.940
Once I have picked the topic I want to use, it's

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.829
time to research. It's amazing how many

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:45.480
scholarly resources you can find with

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.820
just a simple google search, scientific

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.109
journals and study reports are my

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:50.609
favorite resources, because they provide

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:52.469
raw data without imparting significant

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:54.690
bias. I'll take note of the source, and then

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.219
synthesize the information into short

00:00:56.219 --> 00:00:57.960
notes before moving on to the next one.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.059
Once i have enough notes, I'll throw in a

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:01.559
dash of my brilliant Second Thought

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:03.479
brand humor and draft up my script.

00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:04.710
A couple read-throughs to make sure it

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.090
flows nicely, then it's time to record

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.010
the audio. I'm going to let you in on a

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.540
big secret - You don't need a fancy

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.250
recording studio to get decent quality

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.200
audio. Wanna see my studio? Here it is:

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.620
My closet! All the fabric helps absorb

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.300
unwanted noise, which is perfect for

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.430
recording clean audio. As for hardware

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.350
I use a Zoom H5 recorder with a shotgun

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.670
microphone attachment to make sure I only

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.290
capture my voice without any background

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.270
noise. It usually takes me about 20

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.590
minutes to get everything recorded to my

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.750
standards. From there, I'll head over to

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.790
my computer to edit the audio. I'll put a

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.650
link to pcpartpicker.com if you want

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.180
to see the exact parts I used for my build,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.510
but here are the big points: the i7 6700k,

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:43.170
Superclocked GTX 1070, 16 Gigabytes DDR4 RAM

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.320
and a 500 Gigabytes solid-state drive,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.500
operating system and the adobe suite

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.960
Aaand Battlefield.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.030
I'm no sound engineer, but I'm very

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.620
familiar with Adobe Premiere, which is

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.210
adequate for simple trimming of audio files.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.950
I'll take about an hour to go through

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.690
and trim out noticeable breaths and pauses,

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.400
as well as the sound of muffled crying

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.450
when I mess up a sentence for the

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.120
fifteenth time. Once that's done, I'll export it

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.300
as a high-quality MP3 and save it for later.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.020
Now, I have two different ways to make

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.610
the actual video and it all depends on

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.140
how proactive I am in the days leading

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.569
up to the upload. If I'm good, I'll open

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:14.579
my script and Photoshop side-by-side and

00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:15.959
draw the assets I'll need for the video

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:17.700
before I even open AfterEffects.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.000
More often than not though,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.830
I will just draw as I go. In that

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.390
case, I'll have AfterEffects open with

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:23.920
Photoshop minimized and when I need a

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.420
new character or drawing, I will pull up

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:26.830
Photoshop, draw it,

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.870
export it as a PNG file and then import it

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:30.760
into AfterEffects. For drawing, I use a

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.680
Wacom Intuos tablet. It's nothing

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.700
fancy - I think I paid about 80 bucks for it -

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:35.950
but it gets the job done and it's

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.810
never given me any trouble. From there,

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:38.770
I'll just sit in my terribly

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:40.360
uncomfortable walmart chair and edit for

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:41.860
about 10 or 12 hours straight until the

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.440
video is done. And that's it!

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:45.910
It's a lot of hard work, but it's so

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.709
rewarding to finish a video and know you did

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:48.670
a good job.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.320
I'll refine my setup when I'm able and

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.550
hopefully I will be able to put out even

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.739
better videos in the future. Again, I

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:55.060
couldn't do this without the support of

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.220
all you fantastic people. Your comments

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.959
never fail to brighten my day and it's

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:00.160
incredibly rewarding to see our

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.100
community grow the way it has.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.960
Together, we'll make Second Thought one for the

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:04.870
history books!

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:06.400
Remember to ask any questions you have

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:07.920
down below in the comments.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.000
Thanks for watching and I'll see you in the next video!

